On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:52:25 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> We'll put this on ice for now. It's just an idea, thinking out loud.

Understood; I have parked it.

> Is me a notmuch convention, or something built in, or just something you
> came up with?

I came up with it. notmuch has no notion of a current user in tags, and
nothing resolves "me". The only convention is the "::" separator, which
nmbug uses for its shared "notmuch::" prefix. To notmuch-git a prefix is
only a filter: it commits the tags that start with it, and stores them
with the prefix stripped, so "me::archived" lands in the repo as
tags/<hash>/<msgid>/archived.

> Because I wonder how this would work with multiple
> people. Imagine I'm working with a team of people (or agents) and
> everyone has their own archive tag. How does that work? Does me
> automatically resolve to the current user? Or would we need individual
> prefixes like pat, claude, etc.

Neither is needed, because tags are not in the mail. They live in each
reader's own notmuch database. Your database on beastie and mine on
lab-claude share the maildir's messages and nothing else, so when I tag
a message the tag exists only in my database. Each person's notmuch-git
repo is private too. So every reader can use the same tag name, and the
same mail-tag.sh line "-inbox -- tag:me::archived" works for all of
them. "me::" reads as "mine, in whoever's database this is". If you
prefer a plainer name, any fixed prefix works equally well; the choice
only has to keep the personal tags apart from the derived ones.

Per-user prefixes become necessary only if several people share one
tag repo, the way nmbug does. Then everyone pulls everyone's tags, and
the prefix says whose a tag is. That is not what we want for archiving,
which is personal.

> And if we do that - can we stop those
> additional tag prefixes from polluting the UI? I don't care to see
> claude tags.

You will not see them under the scheme above, since my tags never reach
your database. If shared prefixes ever do arrive, notmuch-emacs can hide
them: an entry in notmuch-tag-formats with no expressions hides every
tag its regexp matches. From notmuch-tag.el in notmuch-emacs 0.40, "If
the list is empty, the tag is simply hidden."

    (add-to-list 'notmuch-tag-formats '("claude::.*"))

On the command line, the tags stay visible in notmuch search output;
there is no equivalent filter there.

> See if this version ships it, or where to find it. You have access to
> internet, source code, build tools, etc. Stop acting helpless.

Fair. I fetched the 0.40 release from notmuchmail.org (sha256 matches
the signed checksum file) and read its build and the FreeBSD port.

notmuch 0.40 does ship it, as notmuch-git.py in the top of the source
tree. The default make target copies it to notmuch-git, but upstream's
"make install" installs only the notmuch binary; distributions install
notmuch-git themselves (Debian has debian/notmuch-git.install). The
FreeBSD port mail/notmuch never does, so its pkg-plist has the man
page and no script. That is a port bug, not a missing feature.

The script needs only git and the Python 3 standard library. It
imports notmuch2 if present and falls back without it. "notmuch git"
finds it on PATH as notmuch-git, so a copy in ~/local/bin is enough.

I ran it from the tarball against a scratch copy of the list (its own
NOTMUCH_CONFIG, so neither real database was touched), with
git.tag_prefix=me::. init, commit, and checkout all worked: I tagged
two messages, committed, removed every me:: tag, and checkout restored
both. Two things the man page does not make obvious:

- git.safe_fraction (default 0.1) refuses any commit or checkout that
  changes more than that fraction of the prefixed tags. With a handful
  of archive tags, every change trips it. Set it to 1 for a personal
  archive.
- After a database rebuild there are no prefixed tags, and checkout
  refuses to run until given --force. That is the rebuild step, so it
  reads "notmuch git checkout --force".

The proper fix is in the port. This is the change I would propose for
mail/notmuch; I have not built it, since there is no ports tree here:

    -USES=		gmake gnome pkgconfig python:build,test
    +USES=		gmake gnome pkgconfig python shebangfix
    +SHEBANG_FILES=	notmuch-git.py
    +RUN_DEPENDS=	git:devel/git

    post-install:
    +	${INSTALL_SCRIPT} ${WRKSRC}/notmuch-git ${STAGEDIR}${PREFIX}/bin

    pkg-plist:
    +bin/notmuch-git

It goes inside the ".ifndef MASTERDIR" blocks so that notmuch-emacs
and the other slave ports are unchanged. I can take it through
oss/freebsd-ports, build it with poudriere and file it with the port's
maintainer, if you want it upstream. Until then, a copy of
notmuch-git.py in ~/local/bin is all either of us needs. I have not
installed it, since CLAUDE.md says not to install dependencies; say so
and I will.
